{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 44, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Raymond III served as the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 and he played a pivotal role within the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the twelfth century. His life involved complex political maneuvering and military leadership while he navigated the dangerous landscape of the Crusader states. He emerged as a central figure during the reign of King Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy yet ruled with great determination despite his illness. Raymond acted as regent for the young king on multiple occasions and he sought to maintain stability within the fractious noble classes. His policies often favored negotiation with Muslim powers rather than immediate conflict which placed him at odds with more aggressive factions led by figures such as Raynald of Châtillon.\n\n*Early Life and Succession*\nRaymond inherited his title after his father Raymond II was assassinated by the Order of Assassins in 1152. He assumed control over the County of Tripoli while still a youth and he quickly demonstrated political acumen. The young count faced threats from neighboring Muslim states and he worked to fortify his borders against potential invasion. His marriage to Eschiva of Bures brought him additional lands and influence within the kingdom. This union strengthened his position among the high nobility and allowed him to exert significant sway over royal decisions.\n\n*Role as Regent and Political Struggles*\nThe count served as regent for Baldwin IV during periods when the king was too ill to rule personally. Raymond advocated for peace treaties with Saladin and he opposed reckless military campaigns that could endanger the realm. His cautious approach created tension with the court faction supporting Guy of Lusignan and his wife Sibylla. These internal divisions weakened the kingdom just as external threats grew more severe. Raymond attempted to block Guy from gaining power but his efforts ultimately failed due to shifting alliances and royal decrees.\n\n*Final Years and the Battle of Hattin*\nRaymond made a temporary peace with Saladin before the disastrous Battle of Hattin in 1187. He commanded a portion of the Christian army during this critical engagement but the forces suffered a crushing defeat. Many historians debate his actions during the battle since he escaped while most other leaders were captured or killed. Some accounts suggest he retreated to save his troops while others accuse him of betrayal. He died shortly after the battle possibly from illness or despair over the loss of Jerusalem. His legacy remains mixed due to these controversial events and the ultimate collapse of the kingdom he tried to protect through diplomacy.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -2.479584e-05}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -0.002026882}, {"token": " as", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -3.5762793e-07}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0056447857}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.11094936}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -7.15256e-07}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": " until", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -5.448013e-05}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -2.384186e-07}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -1.0728842e-06}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.0728842e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -7.629424e-06}, {"token": "8", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -4.649173e-06}, {"token": " he", "logprob": -0.0017540331}, {"token": " played", "logprob": -0.15680563}, {"token": " a", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " pivotal", "logprob": -0.089718945}, {"token"